<h2>Why this matters more than a broken link would</h2>

<p>
  A page whose address has changed usually fails loudly: you get an error and you know
  something is wrong. This change does not do that. The address
  <code>jobs/lux/green/</code> still works — but before July it showed Guardrails, the
  most restrictive policy, and now it shows Exposed, the least. Nothing on the page looks
  broken. It simply says the opposite of what it used to.
</p>

<p>
  So if you are comparing an old printout against this site and they disagree, check the
  policy name before assuming the text changed. In almost every case it has not.
</p>

<h2>How to be sure what you are reading</h2>

<p>
  <strong>Read the policy name, not the colour.</strong> Guardrails, Enable, Light-touch
  and Exposed have never moved, and every page and every PDF names one of them. PDFs
  generated after this change also carry the policy name in the footer of every page, so
  a sheet photocopied out of context still identifies itself.
</p>

<p>
  The material from before the change remains available in full as the <code>v5.0.0</code>
  offline archive, so an older reference can always be retrieved rather than reconstructed
  from memory.
</p>

